  "description": "A parametric BEAM on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT. The interior brief asks whether a beam crosses the room and where; the Structural folder had a column and a footing and nothing to answer that question with.\n\nTHE SOFFIT IS THE DATUM, NOT THE TOP. A beam is placed by the height you can walk under and it grows upward from there — so changing the depth never moves the clear height, which is the number the ceiling design actually depends on. `downstand` reports how far the soffit hangs below the slab.\n\nTHE ENCASEMENT IS A BOX AROUND THE BEAM — two cheeks and a soffit board — not a bigger solid. That is what keeps `concreteVolume` the concrete and stops the boarding being quantified as structure.\n\nBEARINGS ARE DRAWN AND SUBTRACTED. The ends that sit on the supports are their own group, and `span` is the length between them, so the clear span and the span-to-depth ratio mean what they say rather than measuring the whole stick.\n\nTHE DROP NIB is the small downstand a curtain pocket or a ceiling change needs. When it is on it becomes the governing clear height, which is why `clearHeight` reads from it rather than from the soffit.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: 3.962 m overall, 0.23 × 0.35 m, soffit 2.70 m under a 3.353 m slab — a 0.653 m downstand, 3.732 m clear span, 0.319 m³ of concrete and 3.69 m² of formwork.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. It is prismatic and straight — no haunch, no taper, no curved or cranked beam. There is no reinforcement: `column-with-math` models bars, this does not. The nib is on one side only.",
